KEYWORDS: Metal−organic framework, metal nanocrystal, water chemistry, ligand design, heterogeneous catalysis
Water adsorption in porous materials is attracting increasing attention because of the scientific interest in the discovery of novel phenomena in confined spaces as well as industrial interests.1−3 Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) have emerged as a new class of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) sorption materials owing to their versatile architectures derived from the numerical combination of the constituting metal ions and organic ligands.4−8 The rational design of MOF architectures based on crystal engineering could appropriately tailor the porous structure according to targeted applications.9−13 Therefore, MOFs have offered various functionalities related to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) for potential applications such as \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) purification,14,15 heat pumps,16 and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) harvesting.17,18 In the aspect of fundamental science, the adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in the MOF nanospaces has afforded unique hydrogen-bonding networks of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) clusters,19,20 characteristic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) dynamics,21,22 inherent proton conductivity,23−26 and enhanced reactivity,27 which could not be feasible in bulk \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\).Furthermore, the pore environment of a MOF is finely customizable through both the substitution of metal ions and the functionalization of organic ligands based on molecular design. Because the pore characteristics could sensitively reflect the associated functionalities of the adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), the fine-tuning of the pore environment in the MOF has been amenable to effectively control various functionalities related to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), including \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) sorption behavior28,29 and proton conductivity.20−22 However, it remains unclear how the customizable pores of MOFs affect the reactivity of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) molecules adsorbed in MOFs. We herein report a systematic control of the reactivity of the 