Furthermore, the pore environment of a MOF is finely customizable through both the substitution of metal ions and
the functionalization of organic ligands based on molecular design. Because the pore characteristics could sensitively reflect the associated functionalities of the adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{:}\) , the finetuning of the pore environment in the MOF has been amenable to effectively control various functionalities related to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{:}\) , including \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) sorption behavior28,29 and proton conductivity.20−22 However, it remains unclear how the customizable pores of MOFs affect the reactivity of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) molecules adsorbed in MOFs. We herein report a systematic control of the reactivity of the adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) by modulating the organic ligands in the MOF for the first time.
The MOF selected for this study is UiO-66 \(\left[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{BDC})_{6}\right.\) \(\left.\left(\mathrm{BDC} = 1.4\text{-benzenedicarboxylate}\right)\right]\), where each zirconium oxide secondary building unit, \(\left[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(-\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}\right.\) is linked to 12 BDC units to form a
porous 3-D framework with small tetrahedral \((6.8\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) and large octahedral \((7.2\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) pores.33 UiO-66 has a robustly thermal- and water-stable structure, and the pore environment is highly designable by the substitution of the metal center from \(Z\mathbf{r}^{4+}\) to \(\mathrm{Hf^{4+}}\) and \(\mathrm{Ce}^{4+34-36}\) and the functionalization of the BDC ligand with various chemical groups such as \(-\mathrm{Br},\ -\mathrm{Me}_{2},\) \(-\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2},\) and \(-\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) .37−39 The tunable pore environment of UiO-66 analogues is expected to modulate the chemical state of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) molecules for controlling the reactivity of the adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) .
To address the reactivity of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) absorbed in MOFs, we focused on water−gas shift (WGS) reaction \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\,+\,\mathrm{CO}\) \(\rightarrow\mathrm{H}_{2}+\mathrm{CO}_{2})\) , in which the activation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) is the rate-determining step of the reaction.40 One of the representative catalysts in the WGS reaction is the Pt nanocrystal (NC),41,42 and we hence covered Pt NCs with UiO-66 \(\left(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\right)\) to control the reactivity of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) by the pores. In this report, we demonstrate systematic control of the WGS reaction activity of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) by changing the functional group of the BDC ligand of UiO-66. The results of solid-state NMR and in situ IR measurements demonstrated that the widely controlled WGS reactivity of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) analogues originates from the differences in the electron density of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in the UiO-66 pores.
Results and Discussion. Stability Test of UiO-66 and the Analogues toward the WGS Reaction. Because the WGS reaction requires harsh reaction conditions, we first examined
the stabilities of UiO-66 and the analogues at high temperature in the presence of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) vapor. We synthesized various UiO-66 analogues by changing metal ions \((Z\mathbf{r}^{4+},\,\mathrm{H}\mathbf{f}^{4+},\) and \({\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}\) ),34−36 the lengths of organic ligands (BDC, fumarate, and biphenyl\(4,\!4^{\prime}\) -dicarboxylate),4 and the functional groups of BDC ligands \(\left(-\mathrm{H},\ -\mathrm{Br},\ -\mathrm{Me}_{2},\ -\mathrm{NH}_{2},\right.\) and \(-\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H})\) .37−39 The stability tests of UiO-66 and the analogues were performed by flowing \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) vapor at \(350\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(0.6\ \mathrm{\MPa}\) (see Supporting Information for details). The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) -treated samples indicated that four of the UiO-66 analogues \(\left(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{H},\right.\) UiO-66-H(Hf), UiO-66-Br, and UiO-66- \(\cdot\mathrm{Me}_{2}\) ) maintained the pristine structures, whereas the structures of the other UiO-66 analogues collapsed (Figures S1−S9). Therefore, we selected four thermally and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) -stable UiO-66 analogues to study the effect of the pore environment on the reactivity of the adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in UiO-66 analogues.
Syntheses and Structural Characterizations of Pt NCs Covered with UiO-66 and the Analogues. We prepared Pt NCs covered with UiO-66-H \(\left(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{H}\right)\) by a facile reactive seeding method, where the Pt NCs acted as seed sites for the MOF growth.43−50 First, Pt NCs were prepared by reducing \((\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}\) with ethylene glycol in the presence of polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) as a capping ligand.51 The average particle size of the synthesized Pt NCs was estimated to be 7.9 \(\pm\ \ 1.0\ \ \mathrm{nm}\) from transmission electron microscopy (TEM)
observations (Figure S10). The obtained Pt NCs were mixed with \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4},\) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC},\) and acetic acid in \(N,N.\) -dimethylformamide (DMF). The mixture was heated at \(110~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) to form \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{H}\) . To investigate the effect of the metal ion of UiO-66 on the WGS reaction, we synthesized \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\) \(\mathrm{H}(\mathrm{Hf})\) using \(\mathrm{HfCl}_{4}\) instead of \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) in the synthetic procedure for \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{H}\) . In addition, to investigate the influence of ligand functionalization of UiO-66, we also synthesized Pt NCs covered with UiO-66 analogues by use of functionalized BDC ligands. The samples functionalized with \(-\mathrm{Br}\) and \(-\mathbf{M}\mathbf{e}_{2}\) groups were named \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{Br}\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{Me}_{2},\) respectively (Figure 1a).
As shown in Figure 1b,c, the PXRD patterns of the obtained \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) analogues consisted of diffractions from both the UiO-66 analogue and Pt lattices. The calculated lattice constants of UiO-66 analogues and Pt were identical to those of pristine corresponding UiO-66 analogues and Pt NCs (Figures S12−S27 and Table S1), suggesting that the crystal structures of UiO-66 analogues and Pt NCs were not changed after their integration. The loadings of Pt included in \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}.\) - 66-H, \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{H}(\mathrm{Hf})\) , \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{Br},\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\) \(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\) were estimated to be 6.7, 4.6, 5.3, and 5.9 wt \(\%,\) respectively, based on inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy measurements. The molar ratio of Pt to \(Z\mathbf{r}\) or \(\mathrm{Pt}\) to Hf is the same for all the \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) analogues \(\left(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Zr}\right.\) or \(\mathrm{Pt/Hf}=0.1)\) . To reveal the composite states of \(\mathrm{Pt}(\boldsymbol{\omega})\) UiO-66 analogues, TEM images were collected. Figure \(\mathbf{1d-g}\) indicates that Pt NCs in \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) analogues were covered entirely with corresponding UiO-66 analogues. The average particle sizes of Pt NCs for \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{H},\) \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\) \(\mathrm{H}(\mathrm{Hf})\) , \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{Br},\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\) were estimated to be \(7.9~\pm~1.0\) , \(8.0~\pm~1.0\) , \(8.1~\pm~1.1\) , and \(8.0~\pm~1.0~\mathrm{\nm},\) respectively, which are consistent with the crystallite sizes estimated by Le Bail fitting for PXRD patterns [8.3(1) nm for \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{H},\) 8.4(2) nm for \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{H}(\mathrm{Hf}),\) 8.1(2) nm for \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{Br},\) and 8.2(1) nm for \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{Me}_{2};\) Table S1]. The similar sizes of Pt NCs in \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) analogues enable appropriate evaluation of the effects of metal replacement and ligand functionalization of UiO-66 on the WGS reactivity of Pt NCs.
Sorption Studies. To investigate the open porosity of \(\mathrm{Pt}(\boldsymbol{\omega})\) UiO-66 analogues, we performed \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption measurements at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\) (Figures S29 and S30). All of the composites exhibited a typical type-I isotherm, indicating the presence of microporosity. Comparing the isotherms of the composites with those of the corresponding pristine UiO-66 analogues, the porosities of UiO-66 analogues are preserved after the
hybridization with Pt NCs (Figures S12, S16, S20, and S24). The replacement of \(Z\mathbf{r}^{4+}\) ions with \(\mathrm{Hf^{4+}}\) ions slightly decreased the uptake amount of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) molecules, probably because of the heavier Hf cations than \(Z\mathbf{r}\) cations (Figure S29).34 On the other hand, the functionalization of BDC ligand with \(-\mathrm{Br}\) and \(-\mathbf{M}\mathbf{e}_{2}\) groups decreased the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) uptake amount due to the smaller pore volumes compared to those of UiO-66-H in the presence of \(-\mathrm{Br}\) and \(-\mathbf{M}\mathbf{e}_{2}\) groups (Figure S30).
Furthermore, to disclose the pore characteristics of \(\mathrm{Pt}(\boldsymbol{\omega})\) UiO-66 analogues, we collected \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) sorption isotherms at 298 K (Figure 2a,b). All of the isotherms displayed a significant step with a hysteresis loop in the course of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) adsorption/ desorption. The step pressures of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{H}\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}.\) - 66-H(Hf) were observed at approximately 0.36 and \(0.32\;P/P_{0},\) respectively, in the adsorption process (Figure 2a). The similar step pressures imply that the affinity of the inner wall of the UiO-66-H to the adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) is not much different in the cases of \(Z\mathbf{r}^{4+}\) and \(\mathrm{Hf^{4+}}\) . In contrast, the step pressure of \(\mathrm{Pt}(\boldsymbol{\omega})\) UiO-66-Br \(\left(0.28\;P/P_{0}\right)\) was lower than that of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{H}\) \(\left(0.36\;P/P_{0}\right)\) , whereas \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\) \(\left(0.45\;P/P_{0}\right)\) exhibited a step pressure higher than that of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{H}\) (Figure 2b). Considering that the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) adsorption behavior in a MOF mainly depends on the surface property such as hydrophobicity/hydrophilicity, these results demonstrate that the functionalization of the BDC ligand with the \(-\mathrm{Br}\) group offered relatively hydrophilic pores and high affinity to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) whereas functionalization with the \(-\mathbf{M}\mathbf{e}_{2}\) group provided relatively hydrophobic pores and low affinity to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) . The difference in the affinity of the UiO-66 to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) by a substitution group is expected to influence the activities of WGS reaction for Pt NCs covered with UiO-66 analogues.
Then, we anticipated that functionalization of the BDC ligand would change the WGS reaction activity because functional groups significantly affect the affinity of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) for UiO-66 of the composites. As shown in Figure \(^{3\mathrm{b},\mathrm{c},}\) \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}-\) 66-Br exhibited a CO conversion of \(21.1\%\) at \(340\ ^{\circ}{\cal C},\) which is 1.7 times lower than that of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO-66-H}\) \((35.6\%)\) ,27 demonstrating that functionalization of the BDC ligand with the \(-\mathrm{Br}\) group reduced the WGS reaction activity. On the other hand, \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO-66-Me}_{2}\) exhibited a CO conversion of \(59.7\%\) at \(340\ ^{\circ}{\cal C},\) which is 1.7 times higher than that of \(\mathrm{Pt}(\boldsymbol{\omega}\) UiO-66-H. The functionalization with \(-\mathrm{Me}_{2}\) caused an enhancement of the reactivity. The trends of the reactivity \(\left(-\mathrm{Me}_{2}\,>\,-\mathrm{H}\,>\,-\mathrm{Br}\right)\) were observed in a wide range of flow rates of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (Figures S34−S36), demonstrating that the WGS reaction activity is systematically controllable through functionalization of the BDC ligands. Considering that the WGS reactivities of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO-66-H},\) \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO-66-Br},\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}(\boldsymbol{\omega}\) UiO-66- \(\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{M}\mathbf{e}_{2}\) are not correlated with the amount of adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (Figure 2b), the reactivity seems to involve the chemical state of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in the UiO-66 analogues rather than a
condensation effect increasing the concentration of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) around Pt NCs by UiO-66 pores.27 In addition, the WGS reaction activity of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO-66-Me}_{2}\) is superior to that of Pt NCs supported on various metal oxides such as Pt on \(Z\mathrm{rO}_{2}\) and Pt on \(\gamma\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) in a wide range of flow rates of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (Figure 3c and Figures S37−S39). We note that pristine UiO-66 analogues exhibited negligible WGS reaction activity (Figure S40), and the composites after the WGS reaction test maintained the crystallinities, mean diameters of Pt NCs, and the porosities of UiO-66 analogues, which were characterized by TEM, PXRD, and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption measurements (Figures S41−S52). Furthermore, the WGS reaction activity of the composites had no obvious change during five cycles (Figure S53), confirming the stability and the recyclability. So far, the attempts to improve the WGS reaction activity of Pt have typically involved the developments of Pt alloys and/or metal oxide support.41,52 This result is the first example that optimization of functional groups in MOFs can improve the WGS reaction activity. It is expected that combining the MOF coating approach and the conventional approaches would contribute to designing highly active catalysts for the WGS reaction.
Chemical State of \(H_{2}O\) Adsorbed in Pt@UiO-66 Analogues. To investigate the chemical state of the adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in UiO-66 analogues in detail, we performed ultrafast \((70\,\mathrm{kHz})\) magic angle spinning (MAS) \(\mathrm{^{1}H}\) solid-state NMR measurements for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) -adsorbed \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO-66}\) analogues at room temperature. In general, ultrafast MAS provides high-resolution \(\mathrm{^{1}H}\) NMR spectra of powder samples53,54 and is an established tool to exploit local structures of MOFs and the adsorbed guest molecules in the pores.55,56 For signal assignment, we also obtained \(\mathrm{^{1}H-^{1}H}\) double-quantum NMR (DQNMR), which reflects protons in solid-state molecules; that is, liquid-like highly mobile protons will be filtered out. In this study, the NMR techniques give valuable information on the chemical state of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) adsorbed in \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO-66}\) analogues. As shown in Figure 4, the high-resolution \(\mathrm{^{1}H}\) NMR spectra of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO-66}\) analogues contain three main regions corresponding to aromatic \((7{-}9\ \mathrm{ppm})\) , adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((3{-}5\ \mathrm{ppm})\) , and \(Z\mathrm{r-OH}\) (−1 to \(2\;\mathrm{ppm}\) ). In addition, \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO-66-Me}_{2}\) contain the signals from the protons of the \(-\mathrm{Me}_{2}\) group. The corresponding DQNMR spectra show that the \(\mathrm{^{1}H}\) signals from the aromatic rings remained, but the signals of adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(Z\mathrm{r-OH}\) are diminished. These observations suggest that the protons of adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(Z\mathrm{r-OH}\) are relatively mobile and potentially exchangeable. It should be noted that the chemical shifts of the adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) molecules depend on the functional groups (3.5, 3.8, and \(3.3\ \mathrm{ppm}\) for \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO-66-}\) H, \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO-66-Br},\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO-66-Me}_{2},\) respectively). Given the fact that a high chemical shift corresponds to a decrease in electron density of the proton, functionalization with the \(-\mathrm{Br}\) group, an electron-withdrawing group, could decrease the electron density of the protons in the adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) compared with unfunctionalized \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO-66}\) . On the other hand, introduction of the \(-\mathrm{Me}_{2}\) group, an electron-donating group, could increase the electron density of protons of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in the pores. As a high electron density of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) is preferable for the dissociation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) to form OH, which is an active intermediate for the WGS reaction,57,58 \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO-66-Me}_{2}\) exhibited the highest WGS reactivity in the three \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO-}\) 66 analogues. In addition, XPS revealed the electronic state modification of \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters by the functionalization of BDC ligands in \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO-66}\) analogues. As shown in Figure S55,
functionalization with the \(-\mathrm{Br}\) group results in a slight oxidation of the constituting \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters relative to that of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\) UiO-66-H, whereas the introduction of \(-\mathbf{M}\mathbf{e}_{2}\) provides a relative reduction of the \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters. Considering that the adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) molecules are diffusive and interact with \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters as well as the organic ligands, the relatively electronrich \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\) may contribute to the increase in the electron density of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in the pores for the enhancement of the WGS reaction activity. We note that the peak positions of UiO-66 analogues without Pt NCs are identical to the corresponding \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) analogues, indicating that incorporating Pt NCs does not primarily affect the electronic state of \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) cluster of UiO-66 analogues (Figures S56−S59).
Reactivity of \(H_{2}O\) in Pt@UiO-66 Analogues in the WGS Reaction. To reveal the formation of OH species resulting from \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) activation, we conducted in situ IR spectra measurements under a mixed gas flow of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{:}\) , CO, and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) at \(300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{H},\) , \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{Br},\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}-\) \(66{\cdot}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\) . In Figure 5, the absorption band at \(3664{-}3668~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) was assigned to \(Z\mathbf{r}{-}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{H}\) species of the \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters.59 In the cases of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{H}\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{Me}_{2},\) the IR band originating from OH species on Pt NCs was observed at \(3734{-}3765~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1},\) , whereas the band was very weak in \(\mathrm{Pt}@\) UiO-66-Br.60 These \(\mathrm{Pt-OH}\) species were not observable under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) gas flow conditions without \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) or in the case of pristine UiO-66 analogues without Pt NCs (Figure S60).
Interestingly, the intensity of the \(\mathrm{Pt-OH}\) band gradually increased in the order \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{Br}<\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{H}<\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{Me}_{2},\) similar to their activity in the WGS reaction. As \(\mathrm{Pt-OH}\) is well-known as an active intermediate in the WGS reaction,60 the tendency of the intensity of the \(\mathrm{Pt-OH}\) bands could address the WGS reactivity in the \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) analogues. In addition, the most active \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{Me}_{2},\) exhibited the absorption band of \(\mathrm{Pt-COOH}\) at \(3533~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , which is an intermediate species formed by \(\mathrm{Pt-CO}\) and \(\mathrm{Pt-}\) OH in the WGS reaction. We note that there is no obvious difference of the \(\mathrm{Pt-CO}\) stretching frequencies in \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) analogues (Figure S61), inferring that the coating of UiO-66 analogues on Pt NCs does not significantly affect the electronic state of the Pt NCs, which is also supported by the results of XPS (Figures S55−S58).61−63 Therefore, the difference of the WGS reactivities is attributable to the different chemical states of the adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in UiO-66 analogues by the ligand functionalization rather than the electron density state of Pt NCs.
In summary, we first demonstrated the systematic control of the reactivity of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) vapor in UiO-66 encapsulating Pt NCs through the WGS reaction. The ligand functionalization of UiO-66 significantly affected the catalytic activity of the WGS reaction \(\mathrm{(Pt@UiO-66-Me_{2}\,>\,P t@U i O-66-H\,>\,P t@U i O-66-}\) H), although the replacement of \(Z\mathbf{r}^{4+}\) with \(\mathrm{Hf^{4+}}\) in UiO-66 had no impact on the catalytic activity because of the identical pore characteristics. The controlled catalytic activity of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) by the ligand functionalization originates from the different chemical states of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in UiO-66. Functionalization with the \(-\mathrm{Br}\) group could decrease the electron density of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) adsorbed in UiO-66 pores, compared with that in unfunctionalized UiO-66. On the other hand, introduction of the \(-\mathbf{M}\mathbf{e}_{2}\) group could increase the electron density of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in the pores. The different chemical state of adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) contributed to the formation of \(\mathrm{Pt-OH}\), an active intermediate in the WGS reaction. This work gives valuable information on the relationship between the chemical state of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) adsorbed in MOFs and the reactivity. In addition, our findings provide a design guideline to develop not only useful WGS reaction catalysts but also various catalysts related to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) by careful selection of the functional group of MOFs.
Methods. Syntheses of \(P t@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}H,\) -Br, and - \(\cdot M e_{2}.\) - \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) ( \(175\;\mathrm{\mg},\) 0.75 mmol), corresponding organic ligands
\(\mathrm{[H_{2}B D C}\) ( \(125~\mathrm{mg},\,0.75~\mathrm{mmol})\) for \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{H};\) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}{\cdot}\mathrm{Br}\) \(\left(184\;\;\mathrm{mg},\;0.75\;\;\mathrm{mmol}\right)\) for \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{Br},\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}{\cdot}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\) \(\left(146~\mathrm{mg},\,0.75~\mathrm{mmol}\right)\) for \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\big]\) and acetic acid (3 mL) were mixed in DMF \(\left(10\,\mathrm{\mL}\right)\) . Then, Pt NCs (0.075 mmol) in DMF \(\left(40\;\mathrm{mL}\right)\) were added and kept at \(110\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 24 h. The dark gray suspensions thus produced were collected and washed with DMF \(\left(3\,\times\,30\,\mathrm{\mL}\right)\) and MeOH \(\left(3\,\times\,30\,\mathrm{\mL}\right)\) using a centrifuge. The white powder was then immersed in MeOH \(\left(30\mathrm{~mL}\right)\) for \(24~\mathrm{{h}}\) . The washing process with MeOH was performed three times and finally immersed in acetone (30 mL) for \(24~\mathrm{{h}}\) . After the washing process, the samples were activated by removing the solvent under vacuum for \(15~\mathrm{h}\) at \(150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) .
Synthesis of \(P t@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}H(H f)\) . \(\mathrm{HfCl}_{4}\) \(\left(240\ \mathrm{~mg},\ \ 0.75\right.\) mmol), \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\) ( \(125~\mathrm{mg},~0.75~\mathrm{mmol})\) , and acetic acid (2 mL) were mixed in DMF \(\left(10\ \mathrm{mL}\right)\) . Pt NCs \(\left(0.075\mathrm{\mmol}\right)\) in DMF \(\left(10\ \mathrm{mL}\right)\) were added and kept at \(110~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{{h}}\) . The dark gray suspensions were washed and activated using the procedure described in the synthesis of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{H}\) .
Gas Sorption Measurements. All samples were activated over \(15~\mathrm{h}\) at \(150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under vacuum. \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) sorption isotherms were measured at 77 and \(298\ \mathrm{K},\) respectively, using volumetric techniques (BELSORP-MAX; Microtrac BEL Corp., Japan).
Water−Gas Shift Reaction. The catalytic activities of the WGS reaction for the prepared composites were evaluated in a fixed-bed flow reactor (BEL-FAE, Microtrac BEL Corp.). The inner diameter of the tubular stainless-steel reactor was \(7~\mathrm{mm}\) . The composites including \(5~\mathrm{mg}\) of \(\mathrm{Pt}\) were placed in the middle of the reactor tube and fixed with glass wool. Then, the catalytic tests were performed with a gas mixture of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (11.4, 57.0, or 114 sccm), CO (10 sccm), and Ar (50 sccm). Deionized \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) was degassed using a degassing device (Gastorr AG-32-01; FLOM Co., Japan) and evaporated at \(180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . The flow rate of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) vapor (11.4, 57.0, or 114 sccm) was controlled by changing the pumping rate of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) liquid (0.01, 0.05, or \(0.10\,\mathrm{\mL/min})\) with a dual pump (KP-21-01; FLOM Co., Japan). The flow rate of CO and Ar was regulated by a mass controller (5850E; Brooks Instrument, USA). The gas mixture of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) , CO, and Ar was preheated at \(150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in a thermostatic bath and introduced in the reactor tube. The reaction temperature was increased by \(20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in the range of \(140{-}340\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with a temperature control device (SDC15; Azbil Co., Japan). The heating rate was \(2~{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min},\) and the reaction time was \(85\,\mathrm{\min}\) at every reaction temperature. The reaction pressure was controlled at \(0.65\;\mathrm{\:MPa}\) by a back pressure regulating valve. The reactant of CO and products such as \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) were analyzed using gas chromatography with a thermal conductivity detector (TCD) (model GC-2014; Shimazu Co., Japan). Activated carbon was used as the TCD column. CO conversion was calculated according to the equation: CO conversion \((\%)~=~(F_{\mathrm{CO~in}}~-~F_{\mathrm{CO~out}})/F_{\mathrm{CO~in}}~\times\) 100, where \(F_{\mathrm{CO\;in}}\) and \(F_{\mathrm{CO\;out}}\) are the inlet and outlet volumes of CO in the reactor, respectively. The consumed amount of CO was identical with the produced amount of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{H}_{2},\) and the calculated CO conversion was the steady-state CO conversion.
Solid-State NMR Measurements. \(^1\mathrm{H}\) magic angle spinning nuclear magnetic resonance (MAS NMR) was performed on a JEOL JNM-ECA600 spectrometer. The sample exposed to the air was put in a sample tube at room temperature. The static magnetic field and MAS frequency were \(14.1\,\mathrm{~T~}\) and \(70~\mathrm{kHz}\) . \(^{1}\mathrm{H}-^{1}\mathrm{H}\) double-quantum filter spectra were obtained with
back-to-back (BaBa) pulse sequence. The recycle delays and recoupling time were \(1{-}8\ s\) and \(89~\mu s,\) respectively.
In Situ IR Spectroscopy. IR spectra were recorded with a Bruker spectrometer, Vertex 70, using a DTGS detector and acquired at \(4~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) resolution. A homemade IR cell allowing in situ treatments in controlled atmospheres and temperatures from 25 to \(500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) was used. For the IR study, the samples were pressed into self-supported wafers and treated in a vacuum \(\left(10^{-4}\right.\) mbar) for \(1.5\,\mathrm{~h~}\) at \(120\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . Afterward, the temperature was increased to the reaction temperature (i.e., \(300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) flow at \(20\,\mathrm{\mL/min}\) . In the in situ WGS studies, the sample was exposed to a flow of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{CO}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) \(\left(4.5/0.4/15.1\ \mathrm{sccm}\right)\) , operating at \(300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) .
